#####################################################################
Introduction
^DRIVE:  Distributed
^         Real-time
^          Interactive 
^           Vehicle 
^            Emulator

DRIVE is a distributed client-server simulation, and the client (which
you are running) must connect to a server.  When it first comes
up, it will search for a server on the local subnet.  It then gives you
the choice of servers to run, or, if one is not found, the option to
start a server on your local system.

After a server is selected, DRIVE creates two windows on your display.
(The placement of these windows may be specified in the
/usr/lib/X11/app-defaults/Drive file).

The larger window (entitled "Driving Simulator") is used for controlling
your vehicle as you drive around in the scene; it also "previews" the
vehicles before you select one.

The smaller window (entitled "Select") allows you to preview the
vehicles available, select a vehicle, select the vehicle's color, and
join the game.  The available vehicles are displayed at the top of the
"Select" window.  To preview a vehicle, simply click on the vehicle's
name using the left mouse button.  The selected vehicle will be
displayed in the large window.  The sliders allow you to modify the
vehicle's color.  To choose a color, simply move the slider bars using
the left mouse button.

Once you have selected the vehicle you want and have chosen a color,
click the "Join" button in the "Select" window to begin driving.  Once
the "Join" button is pressed, the "Select" window will disappear.

When you join the game, you enter the world scene "inside" the vehicle
you have selected.  Your view is from inside the vehicle looking
forward, out the windshield.  There are various keys and buttons you
can press to affect your vehicle or your view -- see the other help
items for details.
.

##############################################################
DRIVE buttons
The buttons at the bottom of the screen control various aspects of the 
simulation.  The 'Windows' button allows you to resize the window to
two preselected sizes.  You can also resize the window through normal
methods provided by your window manager.  On many devices, DRIVE runs
fastest in the smaller window.  The program will try to determine whether
your graphics are fast enough to use a large window; if not, it will 
default to the smaller window.

Also under the 'Windows' menu item is a button to constrain the cursor to
the drive window.  Many users find this makes driving easier, since the 
cursor cannot leave the DRIVE window.  To unconstrain the cursor, simply
select the button again.

Two special windows can be brought up or hidden under the 'Windows' menu
item:  the Radar Window and the Leaders Window.  See other help items
for more details.

The 'Upright' button can be used when you crash and your vehicle is resting
on its side, top, nose, etc.  The button will leave your vehicle where it
is, but in the upright position.  If you are racing, there is a penalty
associated with uprighting yourself:  10% of the total time of the race.

The 'Start Over' button can be used to put your vehicle back to its original
postion.  If you are racing, any checkpoints you have crossed will be
invalidated.

The simulation can be run such that upended vehicles will be automatically
set upright or automatically started over without input from the user.

The 'Quit' button should need no explanation.
.

##############################################################
Input control
The input to the program is assumed to come from a mouse, tablet, joystick,
or other analog device.  The position of the pointer in the screen controls
the vehicle -- when the pointer is near the top of the window, the vehicle
is accelerating; when it's near the bottom, it's braking.  This is reflected
in the "Accelerate/Brake" bar on the side of the main window.  When the 
pointer is left of center, the vehicle has its "wheels" turned left; when
right of center, the wheels are right.  Just like a real car, when you're
in reverse, the sense of left and right are reversed.

The selectors on the input device, such as the buttons on the mouse, are
used to shift gears and to fire the gun (if it is enabled -- see
"Guns").  The left button shifts the gearshift down; the right button,
up.  To shift into reverse, press the left button until the the gear
selector shows 'R'.

As a convenience, by pressing the 'c' key, you can center the pointer in
the screen.

The keyboard is also used for input -- see the other subjects for details.
.

##############################################################
drive_server
DRIVE is a client-server application.  A central process, the
"drive_server", controls the physics and interactions of the world.
Connected to the drive_server can be up to 32 "drive clients", which do
all the graphics for each driver in the simulation.  The two processes
communicate across the LAN via IPC sockets.

The drive_server can be configured in many ways -- from running
different racecourses and allowing the drivers to use "Turbo boosts"
and/or guns, to race details and driving at night.  The server can exist
on any HP machine, though the closer on the LAN, the less chance of
lost frames due to LAN delays.

The server puts out "graphics frames" at a high rate:  about the same
as motion picture film.  Each client picks these frames off the net as
fast as it can draw them.  Thus, both fast graphics machines and slow
graphics machines stay "synchronized" in the world, but fast machines
will be displaying more frames per second and will have a smoother
sense of motion.
.

##############################################################
Races
Various racecourses are included with DRIVE.  Races are controlled
by the drive_server.  The person running the server can select from
various racecourses; change their timing; and change their parameters.

Races are run by running from checkpoint to checkpoint on a racecourse.
Depending on the configuration of the drive_server, your vehicle is timed
either from the start of a race or from when it crosses the Starting Line.
Races end either when a timer runs out and/or when someone crossed the
Finish Line (depending on the server).  The vehicle that crossed the 
highest number of consecutive checkpoints in the least amount of time
is the winner.
.

##############################################################
Views
By pressing certain keys, you can "turn your head" and look out the sides
and rear of your vehicle.
^  The 'f' key (Forward) looks out the front of the vehicle.
^  The 'a' key (Aft) looks out the rear of the vehicle.
^  The 's' key (Sinister) looks left.
^  The 'd' key (Dexter) looks right.

When in Skycam Mode (q.v.), these keys move the camera differently.
.

##############################################################
Skycam Mode
This is a mode that allows you to see from a different perspective the
world around your vehicle.  When in Skycam Mode, you end up always
looking toward your vehicle, but you can control the angle at which you
view it, as well as the distance of the viewpoint from the vehicle.
Thus, a great deal of the scene around your vehicle may be viewed,
depending on how you position the viewpoint.

To enter Skycam Mode, press the 'v' key or the 'Home' key.  The
camera can be positioned with the arrow keys and other keys:
^  The left arrow and the 's' key rotate the camera left.
^  The right arrow and the 'd' key rotate the camera right.
^  The down arrow and the 'a' key rotate the camera down.
^  The up arrow and the 'f' key rotate the camera up.
^  The 'Next' key and the 'i' key move the camera closer to the vehicle.
^  The 'Previous' key and the 'o' key move the camera further from the vehicle.

To exit Skycam Mode, press the 'Home' or 'v' key again.
.
##############################################################
Turbo
Pressing the 't' key (for "Turbo") will give your vehicle a burst of power.
Races can be configured to run with no turbo, limited turbo, and unlimited
turbo.  With limited turbo, you get a limited number of presses of the 't'
key per race -- use them wisely.  With unlimited turbo, you can use as much
turbo as you like, though at high speeds vehicles may be difficult to 
control.
.

##############################################################
Guns
The drive_server can be set to run with guns in several modes.
With guns disabled you cannot fire.  With them enabled, the server
can be set so that there is a visual explosion when a shell impacts
something.  In addition, it can be set to throw vehicles away from the
impact point, or to reset any vehicle hit.  To fire a shell, press the
middle button.

When guns are enabled, you can get one of two special "gunsight view"'s,
complete with crosshair, by pressing 'g'.  The first time 'g' is pressed,
a crosshair is drawn in the main view window.  If 'g' is pressed again,
a telescopic view, which can be zoomed in or out with the 'Prev' and 'Next'
keys, is displayed in the main view window.
You can also direct your attention
to the other views such as left and right while in "Gunsight Mode", but
when looking anywhere but where the gun is actually pointed, the
crosshairs will not be drawn.  Pressing 'g' once more will return the
main view window to its original state (with no crosshairs displayed).

The tank can actually move its gun and turret independent of the vehicle.
When the 'Shift' key is pressed, the wheel and accelerator are locked in
their current position, and the mouse position controls the aiming of the
gun:  up, down, left, and right, as you would expect.  No other vehicle
can aim its gun independent of the vehicle body.

MINES can be dropped behind a vehicle when guns are enabled; just hold 
down the 'Shift' key and press the middle button.  Mines dropped from
a vehicle will be active for 10 seconds, then dissappear. Any object 
running into a mine will set off an explosion, with similar effects
as described above for shells.
.

##############################################################
Night Driving
The drive_server can be set up to run so that everything looks like a
night view.  When running at night, headlights become important.  The
'l' key (for "Lights") turns your headlights on and off.  There is also
a highbeam/lowbeam control toggled by the 'h' key.

Headlights should be left off during the day, because on many machines
performance will be reduced when headlights are on.
.

##############################################################
Space Vehicles
Certain vechicle types (X Fighter, for example) have significantly different
controls than the normal car vehicles.  To control these space vehicles,
use the following:
^ Mouse Left/Right : Turn left and right
^ Mouse Down:   Pull nose of vehicle up (climb)
^ Mouse Up:     Push nose of vehicle down (dive)
^ Mouse Buttons  Left: Roll Left  Right: Roll Right  Middle: Fire
^  (Use Shift+Mouse buttons for faster roll)
^ 1-0 for 10-100% throttle
^ ` for 0% throttle 
^ z,x:  Increment / Decrement throttle

These Vehicles are anti-gravity, rear thrust propulsion.  If the engines are
shut completely off, the vehicle will slowly sink to the ground.  Otherwise,
the vehicle ignores gravity.
.

##############################################################
Radar Window
Under the 'Windows' menu item in the menu bar at the bottom of the main
window is a button to turn the "Radar" window on and off.  At the center of
the Radar Window is a dot representing your vehicle.  The Radar Window
shows the positions of all other vehicles in the simulation.  Distances in
the Radar Window are not linear -- the distance of a dot from the center of
the window is proportional to the square root of the distance of the
corresponding vehicle to your vehicle.
.

##############################################################
Dual Radar Window
If Dual Radar is specified in the .driverc file, a '3 Dimensional' radar
will be used.  The 3D radar has two windows:  The upper window is 
the normal ground (X-Z) plane radar, showing the position of vehicles 
position relative to the way your vehicle is facing; front, back, left
and right positions are shown.  In the lower window, the X-Y plane is
shown, indicating whether the vehicles are above or below you (again
depending on the way you are facing) as well as if they are to your left
or right.  Additionally, a wireframe representation of the ground plane
is drawn -- in red if you are pointing towards the ground, and in green
if you are facing away from the ground.  With these two radar windows,
you should have a fairly good idea of where your vehicle is with respect
to other vehicles and the ground.
.

##############################################################
Leaders Window
Under the 'Windows' menu item in the menu bar at the bottom of the main
window is a button to turn the "Leaders" window on and off.  The Leaders
Window shows the current race standings.  Each time a vehicle in a race
goes through a checkpoint, the standings are updated.
.

##############################################################
Watch Mode
When in Watch Mode, the user is not actually controlling a vehicle; he
or she is watching another driver as if he were a passenger in the
other driver's vehicle.  All of the keys that control the view work
correctly in Watch Mode, but the user cannot steer or otherwise affect
the car.

When in Watch Mode, by pressing the "Next Vehicle" button, the user can
change the point-of-view to be that of the next vehicle in the
simulation.

When in Automatic Watch Mode, the point-of-view automatically switches
to the next vehicle every few seconds.
.

##############################################################
driverc
Many of the parameters of DRIVE can be controlled through the ".driverc"
file in the user's HOME directory.  Here is a list of options and choices
for the .driverc file:
^  Vehicle:  {any vehicle name}
^    The default vehicle is selected.
^  Color: {0.0-1.0} {0.0-1.0} {0.0-1.0}
^    By specifying red, green, and blue values between 0.0 and 1.0, the
^    default color is set.  Very dark colors will be mapped to lighter
^    colors, since dark colors do not show up on the Radar Window.
^  Colorname:  {X color name}
^    You can also specify the color with any X Named Color (see rgb.txt).
^  Autostart
^    The simulation will not go through vehicle and color selection,
^    it will use the defaults.
^  Confine Cursor
^    The simulation will start with the cursor confined to the main window.
^  Small Window
^    The simulation will always start in a small window.
^  Radar
^    The Radar Window should be mapped initially.
^  Server: {hostname}
^    Specifies the default place to look for a running drive_server.
^  Nickname: {string}
^    The name that should be displayed for your vehicle when the "Who"
^    key is pressed.
^  Watch Mode
^    DRIVE should start up in Watch Mode (q.v.).
^  Automatic Watch Mode
^    DRIVE should start up in Automatic Watch Mode.
^  Standings
^    The Leader Standings Window should be mapped initially.
^  Dual Radar
^    Turns on the Dual Radar mode. (See the Dual Radar help section)
^
^Several options are only used in Watch Mode:
^  Automatic Watch Frames:  {framecount}
^    The number of frames before switching to a new vehicle.
^  Automatic Camera
^    Camera movements are automated.
^  Overhead Camera
^  Camera Up Angle: {0-90}
^    Elevation angle of the camera in SkyCam Watch Mode.
^  Camera Around Angle: {0-359}
^    Azimuth of the camera in SkyCam Watch Mode.
^  Camera Distance:  {distance}
^    Distance to the camera in SkyCam Watch Mode.
.

##############################################################
Command-Line Options
The drive program is run as follows:  drive [-w|-W] [hostname].
^  The -w option starts the program in Watch Mode.
^  The -W option starts the program in Automatic Watch Mode.
^  "hostname" specifies where the drive_server is running.
.

